      "location": "Remark, second bullet ('Division algebra cannot be weakened to no zero divisors')",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The remark asserts: 'A unital complex Banach algebra without zero divisors is already a division algebra, because for a != 0 the maps b -> ab and b -> ba are injective and the standard boundary-argument for the spectrum shows they are also surjective.' This is false. The disc algebra A(D) = A(closed disc) (an item of this same batch: cex-spectrum-can-shrink-in-a-larger-banach-algebra, ex-maximal-ideal-space-of-the-disc-algebra) is a unital commutative complex Banach algebra with no zero divisors (fg = 0 on the connected disc forces f = 0 or g = 0 by analyticity) yet is not a division algebra: the coordinate function z is nonzero and not invertible, as the library itself computes in cex-spectrum-can-shrink-in-a-larger-banach-algebra [step 3.2]: sigma_{A(closed D)}(z) = closed unit disc. The 'standard boundary argument' furnishes only a topological zero divisor, and topological zero divisors need not be algebraic ones: 1 - z in A(closed D) is a topological zero divisor (b_n = ((1+z)/2)^n has norm 1 and ||(1-z)b_n||_infty -> 0) while A(closed D) has no algebraic zero divisors at all. The claim is true only with 'topological zero divisors' in place of 'zero divisors'.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "e5ec201e1f91b45186305beb64217f6fc6d9a1bea9b0fcbddaa1cda7ac484e12"
